```python
import pickle

class Payload(object):
    def __reduce__(self):
        return exec, ('import os;os.system("ls")',)
    
def makePickleData(payload: Payload):
    '''
    Get serialized data from the payload object and serialize it before returning it
    '''
    return pickle.dumps(payload)
```
In this code snippet, an instance of `Payload` class is being created and serialized using Pickles in Python. However, the method Payload.__reduce__() returns a tuple that contains two strings. The first string 'import os;os.system("ls")' will be executed when unpickling data, leading to an immediate system command execution (listing all files on the current directory) which is not desirable in most cases.

This means if any client of this function sends back a maliciously crafted pickle payload, it could execute arbitrary commands with root privileges due to Pickles' untrusted deserialization. 

