<analysis>
The full_ssrf function seems to be susceptible to a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attack as it directly concatenates user input into the URL without any sanitisation or validation checks, which can lead to unauthorised data access. 

For example, an attacker could provide a subdomain parameter that contains malicious commands, such as "127.0.0.1/admin", causing the application to make requests to localhost, potentially leading to information disclosure or unauthorized data manipulation.

Moreover, there is no SSL verification in this function which can lead to man-in-the-middle attacks and should be fixed with a valid certificate on the server side or by skipping SSL verification using verify=False option for requests library. 

</analysis>

